  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nLead upper level disturbance and cold pool aloft may bring\nisolated to scattered, mainly afternoon showers/thunderstorms\ntoday, with best chances across higher terrain areas mainly west\nof the Hudson River. Slow storm movement could allow for locally\nheavy downpours to occur.\n\nAs main upper level trough approaches from west Tuesday,\nincreasingly upper level diffluent flow and low level\nconvergence will allow for greater coverage of\nshowers/thunderstorms, especially during the afternoon and\nevening hours. Nearly uni-directional low/mid level flow will\nallow for some training of showers/thunderstorms, leading to\npotential locally heavy rainfall, especially across the SE\nCatskills and mid Hudson Valley/NW CT regions, as well as across\nportions of the Mohawk Valley and SW Adirondacks. Latest HREFs\nsuggest 3-hour probs for >1\" rainfall as high as 70-90% across\nthe SW Adirondacks/Mohawk Valley region between 21Z/Tue-00Z/Wed,\nand 30-70% elsewhere. Similar 3-hour probs for >3\" rainfall are\n10-30% across SW Adirondacks/Mohawk Valley and SE Catskills/mid\nHudson Valley after 18Z/Tue, highlighting areas with greatest\nchances of heaviest rainfall.\n\nFlash Flood guidance is generally quite high across the region\ndue to fairly dry antecedent conditions, with 1 hour FFG\ngenerally 1.5-2.5\", and 3-hour FFG generally 2.5\"-3.5\", highest\nfor areas north of I-90. As such, despite the locally heavy\nrainfall potential, overall high FFG suggests mainly isolated to\nscattered instances of flash flooding expected (Marginal to\nSlight Risk per WPC), with greatest chances mainly south of\nI-90 where FFG is slightly lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDigging upper level trough is expected to develop into a closed\nlow somewhere across central/southern NY or northern PA \nWednesday before slowly advancing east southeast Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. Depending on exactly where this sets up, and \nresulting surface features track, bands of moderate to heavy \nrainfall may pivot north and west into the region Wednesday into\nThursday. Rainfall should be mainly stratiform, although some \nelevated instability could allow for some embedded convective \nelements.\n\nOverall rainfall amounts of 1-3\" are currently expected from\ntoday through Thursday morning, with greatest amounts across the\nSE Catskills due to enhancement from upslope flow. Locally\nhigher amounts of 3-4\" could occur in these areas, and any other\nlocations where heavier rainbands set up and stall and/or pivot.\n\nDespite this heavy rainfall, overall groundwater storage\ncapacity remains fairly high across the region, which should\nallow the ground to soak it up with low chances for river\nflooding. Latest MMEFS (ensemble river forecasts) do not suggest\nany river flooding in eastern NY/western New England assuming\nwidespread rainfall amounts remain under 4-5\". So, although\nlocalized ponding of water in some urban/poor drainage areas,\nand isolated/scattered instances of possible flash flooding\nwhere any training heavy downpours occur, the threat for river\nflooding appears very low at this time.",
